    ("h1", "Abstract"),
    ("p", "Indian Railways sells reserved seats through one of the busiest digital ticketing platforms in the world. The hard problem at this scale is not the booking form; it is coordination. When a large burst of buyers targets the same seat, an implementation that checks availability and then writes the booking in a separate step can allow more than one buyer to pass the check before either writes. The result is a double booking, a refund, a support ticket and a loss of trust."),
    ("p", "RailReserve is a small distributed system that isolates this problem and proves a single invariant: **exactly one active hold may exist for a seat, and a paid hold may produce exactly one booking and one payment.** Two stateless FastAPI replicas sit behind Nginx and share a Postgres 16 primary, which is the authority for inventory. A deliberately naive read-delay-write endpoint demonstrates the race. The safe path resolves it with one conditional `UPDATE` statement plus a partial unique index on active holds. Payment is idempotent through unique idempotency keys, and a hold TTL plus a background sweeper returns abandoned seats to inventory. Redis is used only for fail-open per-user rate limiting and is deliberately kept off the booking critical path."),
    ("p", "The implementation is supported by fifteen automated tests and a six-scenario command-line driver. In the evaluated run, twenty concurrent safe hold attempts produced one `201 Created` and nineteen `409 Conflict` responses. The naive path produced twenty winners in the command-line run, while the browser showed six winners because of its per-host connection limit. Two hundred attempts across fifty seats produced fifty winners on fifty distinct seats. Ten concurrent payments with one idempotency key produced one booking and one payment. A forced-expired hold was reclaimed, and Nginx continued to serve health and booking requests when one API replica was stopped. The report maps each claim to its evidence and states the limits of the demonstration."),
