    ("h1", "2. Problem statement and scope"),
    ("h2", "2.1 The double-booking race"),
    ("p", "A seat hold is a classic check-then-act sequence. The application reads the seat, decides that it is available, performs some work (for example, waiting for the user to complete checkout) and then writes the booking. When two requests run at the same time, both can read `available` before either writes. The delay does not create the bug; it only widens the window in which the bug is easy to observe. The real defect is that the check and the write are separate operations with no shared atomic boundary."),
    ("p", "A process-local mutex cannot fix this once the application has more than one replica. Each replica has its own memory, so two replicas can each take their own lock and both proceed. A correct solution must coordinate at a point that all writers share: the database row, a distributed lock service, or a serialized queue. RailReserve chooses the database because it already holds the authoritative inventory and can make the decision durable in the same transaction."),
    ("p", "The naive path in this project deliberately performs the read, sleeps for a configurable window (`NAIVE_DELAY_MS`, set to 300 ms in Compose) and then inserts a record and marks the seat booked without a guard. It is a **teaching control**, not a product feature. The safe path performs one conditional `UPDATE`; Postgres takes a row lock, the second writer waits, and when it re-evaluates the predicate the seat is no longer available. Figure 1 contrasts the two timelines."),
    ("figure", {
        "file": "assets/race-timeline.png",
        "caption": "Check-then-act race versus an atomic conditional update. The naive path lets both requests believe they won; the safe path makes Postgres the arbiter.",
        "width": 6.4,
    }),
    ("h2", "2.2 Problem statement"),
    ("p", "Given **N concurrent purchase attempts for the same seat**, the system must ensure that **at most one active hold and at most one booking commit**, while keeping the response predictable, making payment retries safe, releasing abandoned inventory, and preserving service when an API replica fails. The system must also expose enough evidence to show that the guarantee holds under a real concurrent workload rather than only in a single-threaded test."),
    ("h2", "2.3 Proposed solution"),
    ("p", "RailReserve is a containerized, two-replica booking service. The browser dashboard talks to Nginx, which distributes requests across two FastAPI replicas. Both replicas use the same Postgres primary. The safe hold endpoint runs one conditional `UPDATE` that changes the seat from `available` (or an expired `held`) to `held`, returning the row only to the winner. A partial unique index on `holds (seat_id) WHERE status = 'active'` is a second, schema-level guard. Payment inserts a booking, a payment and the seat transition in one transaction, with unique idempotency keys. A sweeper releases expired holds, and Nginx retries a failed upstream so one dead replica does not take the service down."),
    ("p", "The naive endpoint is kept beside the safe endpoint so that the two behaviours can be compared in the same deployment. The dashboard and the CLI driver use the same HTTP API, so the visual demonstration and the automated evidence are testing the same code path."),
